WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.368 --> 00:00:07.630
RUWEN HESS: Hello, and
welcome to the session,

00:00:07.630 --> 00:00:10.480
Best Practices for Block
Storage on Google Cloud.

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:12.710
My name's Ruwen Hess,
and I'm a product manager

00:00:12.710 --> 00:00:14.420
on Compute Engine Team.

00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:16.420
And today, I have some
exciting new developments

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:19.220
on block storage
to share with you.

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:22.220
Before we jump in, I have a
short safe harbor statement.

00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:25.010
The purpose of this presentation
is to provide better visibility

00:00:25.010 --> 00:00:27.230
into our current product
plans and contains

00:00:27.230 --> 00:00:28.595
forward-looking statements.

00:00:28.595 --> 00:00:31.520
While we strive to meet
the timelines you will see,

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:33.200
this is not an
official commitment,

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:35.090
but our current best estimate.

00:00:35.090 --> 00:00:38.510
Products on our roadmap and
any products in early release

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:41.210
are not guaranteed to
become generally available,

00:00:41.210 --> 00:00:45.580
or may be significantly modified
from their current form.

00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:47.950
In this session, we'll see
an overview of block storage

00:00:47.950 --> 00:00:51.370
on Google Cloud with details
of what is new this year.

00:00:51.370 --> 00:00:53.380
We cover available
disk types and discuss

00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:56.470
how the new disk types fit
into the product family.

00:00:56.470 --> 00:00:58.900
We then dive deeper into
how to select the right disk

00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:01.540
types for your workloads to
better optimize performance

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:03.340
and cost.

00:01:03.340 --> 00:01:05.489
Finally, we'll
cover some details

00:01:05.489 --> 00:01:07.290
of how throttling
and performance works

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:12.610
for block storage, ending with
some simple best practices.

00:01:12.610 --> 00:01:15.240
This year, we're expanding
our Persistent Disk product

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:18.160
portfolio with two new desktops.

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:21.250
Balanced PD is our general
purpose disk, best suited

00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:24.730
for enterprise applications.

00:01:24.730 --> 00:01:26.480
And for the highest
performance workloads,

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:30.230
such as SAP HANA and other
high-end in-memory databases,

00:01:30.230 --> 00:01:32.910
we're introducing Extreme PD.

00:01:32.910 --> 00:01:35.730
At the same time, all of
our existing disk types

00:01:35.730 --> 00:01:39.920
are seeing substantial increases
in performance this year.

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:41.980
We have increased the
maximum sustained read IOPS

00:01:41.980 --> 00:01:44.530
for zonal SSD persistent disks.

00:01:44.530 --> 00:01:49.820
You can now issue 100,000 IOPS
per second, up from 60,000

00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:50.820
previously.

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:53.410
IOPS-sensitive workloads
should see substantial benefits

00:01:53.410 --> 00:01:55.032
from this increase.

00:01:55.032 --> 00:01:57.240
We've also increased the
maximum sustained throughput

00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:01.470
for zonal persistent disks
from 1,200 megabytes reads

00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:04.390
and 400 megabytes writes.

00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:06.130
Five times the sustained
lead throughput

00:02:06.130 --> 00:02:07.733
enables cost-effective
implementation

00:02:07.733 --> 00:02:09.650
of large scale throughput
intensive workloads,

00:02:09.650 --> 00:02:11.450
such as Hadoop.

00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:13.680
Some workloads are
optimized for TCO.

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:16.370
Others need every bit of
performance they can get.

00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:19.190
Google Compute Engine now offers
a wider spectrum of disk types

00:02:19.190 --> 00:02:21.410
to match your workload
needs, providing you

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:25.010
with the same flexibility that
our VM families already offer.

00:02:25.010 --> 00:02:26.720
With the new disk
types, you can pick

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:29.280
the right price/performance
profile for your workload

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:30.870
storage needs.

00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:35.040
Let's look at some of these
changes a little more closely.

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:37.500
Generally available
this month, Balanced PD

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:40.610
is our new general purpose
disk for Google Compute Engine.

00:02:40.610 --> 00:02:43.440
It offers SSD performance
and represents an over 40%

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:45.450
saving compared
to Performance PD,

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:48.730
offering the lowest TCO for
general purpose workloads.

00:02:48.730 --> 00:02:50.370
Balanced PD has the
right performance

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:52.530
for most enterprise
apps, web servers,

00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:55.140
and even some small
and medium databases.

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:57.360
It also offers many of
the same advanced features

00:02:57.360 --> 00:02:59.250
you already know
from Performance PD.

00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:01.530
You can create regional
or zonal volumes,

00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:03.150
and you can attach
multiple instances

00:03:03.150 --> 00:03:05.860
to a single volume
in beat-only mode.

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:09.610
Balanced PD replaces Standard PD
as a new general purpose disk,

00:03:09.610 --> 00:03:13.230
with six IOPS per gigabyte and
a maximum of 80,000 sustained

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:16.120
IOPS, it offers significantly
higher performance

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:18.140
and more performance per dollar.

00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:21.480
In fact, whether write-heavy,
read-heavy, or in-between,

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:24.220
Balanced PD offers
two times the IOPS

00:03:24.220 --> 00:03:27.550
per dollar for most
use cases or better.

00:03:27.550 --> 00:03:31.000
This means that despite the
higher cost per gigabyte,

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:34.090
for many workloads, Balanced
PD can actually to improve TCO

00:03:34.090 --> 00:03:36.550
when you take into account
the impact of faster

00:03:36.550 --> 00:03:39.610
performance at the
workload level end to end.

00:03:39.610 --> 00:03:42.460
This can express itself in
the form of cost savings

00:03:42.460 --> 00:03:44.140
from faster task completion.

00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:47.920
For example, reduced Dataproc
costs, or in the reduced need

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:51.830
to size to IOPS rather
than pure capacity.

00:03:51.830 --> 00:03:55.280
Performance PD is our
new name for PD-SSD,

00:03:55.280 --> 00:03:57.080
as it continues to
evolve and focus

00:03:57.080 --> 00:03:59.360
on performance-sensitive
workloads.

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:01.640
This year, we have already
increased read IOPS

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:03.620
from 60,000 to 100,000.

00:04:03.620 --> 00:04:05.570
And this month, we
are following this up

00:04:05.570 --> 00:04:08.360
by increasing write IOPS
for zonal Performance PD

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:11.130
from 30,000 to 100,000 as well.

00:04:11.130 --> 00:04:12.980
Similarly, this
month we are also

00:04:12.980 --> 00:04:15.470
increasing write throughput
from 800 to 1,200 megabytes

00:04:15.470 --> 00:04:16.510
per second.

00:04:16.510 --> 00:04:19.250
And so zonal Performance PD
will have full read/write parody

00:04:19.250 --> 00:04:21.029
coming forward.

00:04:21.029 --> 00:04:23.880
Customers running databases
with write-heavy workloads

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:26.233
should see noticeable
improvements in performance,

00:04:26.233 --> 00:04:27.900
from a three-fold
increase in write IOPS

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:30.810
and 50% improvement
in write throughput.

00:04:30.810 --> 00:04:35.635
And Performance PD continues to
have the best price per IOPS.

00:04:35.635 --> 00:04:37.760
We will also continue to
add more advanced features

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:39.050
to Performance PD.

00:04:39.050 --> 00:04:42.500
And so in 2021, in addition
to the existing multi-reader

00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:44.960
feature, we will offer
a multi-writer feature

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:46.590
on this disk type.

00:04:46.590 --> 00:04:50.690
We expect to have this ready
for preview later this year.

00:04:50.690 --> 00:04:53.400
For use cases that need
more IOPS or more throughput

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:58.380
than Performance PD offers,
we're introducing Extreme PD.

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:00.300
Extreme PD is our
new disk type for

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:03.030
high-end performance-critical
applications.

00:05:03.030 --> 00:05:04.770
It has been designed
with SAP HANA

00:05:04.770 --> 00:05:07.650
and other high-end
in-memory databases in mind.

00:05:07.650 --> 00:05:11.580
At launch, it will offer
120,000 provisioned IOPS and 2.4

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:14.610
gigabytes a second of
maximum sustained throughput.

00:05:14.610 --> 00:05:17.100
Extreme PD introduces
provision IOPS,

00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:19.230
allowing you to provision
capacity and performance

00:05:19.230 --> 00:05:20.310
separately.

00:05:20.310 --> 00:05:23.250
Provisioned IOPS are
specified at disk creation

00:05:23.250 --> 00:05:25.560
and then can be changed
dynamically at any time,

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:27.690
allowing you to adjust
disk performance

00:05:27.690 --> 00:05:31.150
to the specific requirements
of your workload over time.

00:05:31.150 --> 00:05:34.180
Next year, there will also
be explicit performance SLAs

00:05:34.180 --> 00:05:36.500
for provisioned IOPS.

00:05:36.500 --> 00:05:40.220
Extreme PD is preview
available this month,

00:05:40.220 --> 00:05:41.900
and general
availability is expected

00:05:41.900 --> 00:05:45.220
to follow in the third quarter.

00:05:45.220 --> 00:05:48.930
Finally, we've also continued
to improve local SSD.

00:05:48.930 --> 00:05:52.050
Local SSD now offers more
than three times the IOPS--

00:05:52.050 --> 00:05:55.740
2.4 million read and
1.2 million write IOPS.

00:05:55.740 --> 00:06:00.120
And local SSD now supports 9
terabytes, a threefold increase

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:02.280
in maximum capacity.

00:06:02.280 --> 00:06:05.040
For Flash-optimized databases,
performance-sensitive

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:07.980
analytics, or hot
caches, Local SSD

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:10.050
now offers industry-leading
performance

00:06:10.050 --> 00:06:13.260
per dollar for local storage.

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:15.240
With a new disk type
and improved performance

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:17.130
for existing disk
types, you can pick

00:06:17.130 --> 00:06:19.990
the right price/performance
profile for your workload.

00:06:19.990 --> 00:06:24.380
Let's have a quick look at
choosing the right disk type.

00:06:24.380 --> 00:06:26.360
For many workloads,
three criteria

00:06:26.360 --> 00:06:29.090
help to quickly identify
a good starting point

00:06:29.090 --> 00:06:30.920
for the right disk type.

00:06:30.920 --> 00:06:33.410
Firstly, what does the
price/performance profile look

00:06:33.410 --> 00:06:34.340
like?

00:06:34.340 --> 00:06:37.130
What are your workload's I/O
and throughput requirements?

00:06:37.130 --> 00:06:40.650
How sensitive is the workload
to storage, scale, and cost?

00:06:40.650 --> 00:06:43.070
Second, what are the
availability and durability

00:06:43.070 --> 00:06:44.210
requirements?

00:06:44.210 --> 00:06:47.060
If the workload is replicating
data across instances,

00:06:47.060 --> 00:06:49.400
is this being handled
at the app, database,

00:06:49.400 --> 00:06:50.850
or storage layer?

00:06:50.850 --> 00:06:55.760
Finally, what storage
capacity will be needed?

00:06:55.760 --> 00:06:58.220
Looking at performance
in a bit more detail,

00:06:58.220 --> 00:07:02.060
the right price and right
price/performance by workload

00:07:02.060 --> 00:07:03.703
depends on the disk type.

00:07:03.703 --> 00:07:05.120
The exact performance
requirements

00:07:05.120 --> 00:07:07.880
will, of course, vary for
each specific workload.

00:07:07.880 --> 00:07:09.860
But in general, the
type of workload

00:07:09.860 --> 00:07:12.230
will be a good indicator
of which disk type is

00:07:12.230 --> 00:07:13.970
likely to be a good fit.

00:07:13.970 --> 00:07:15.830
Workloads that are
performance-critical,

00:07:15.830 --> 00:07:17.810
that depend on the highest
available throughput

00:07:17.810 --> 00:07:20.480
and/or IOPS and need
durability are likely

00:07:20.480 --> 00:07:23.000
best served by Extreme PD.

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:26.750
An example of this might
be in-memory databases.

00:07:26.750 --> 00:07:28.770
IOPS-driven
performance-sensitive workloads

00:07:28.770 --> 00:07:32.550
like most larger databases are
a good fit for Performance PD.

00:07:32.550 --> 00:07:36.400
Performance PD offers
the best price per IOPS.

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:40.090
Balanced PD offers substantially
lower TCO than performance PD,

00:07:40.090 --> 00:07:42.380
and offers the right performance
for web serving, line

00:07:42.380 --> 00:07:45.290
of business, and IT apps.

00:07:45.290 --> 00:07:47.330
Finally, Standard PD
is like a good fit

00:07:47.330 --> 00:07:49.670
for throughput-oriented,
cost-sensitive workloads

00:07:49.670 --> 00:07:52.340
with substantial
capacity requirements

00:07:52.340 --> 00:07:54.320
such as, for example,
Hadoop or Kafka.

00:07:57.240 --> 00:07:59.760
Similarly, durability and
availability requirements

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:01.730
will vary by workload.

00:08:01.730 --> 00:08:04.280
As it is ephemeral, Local
SSD is a good option

00:08:04.280 --> 00:08:07.100
for stateless workloads
where replication is managed

00:08:07.100 --> 00:08:09.830
at the app or database layer.

00:08:09.830 --> 00:08:12.770
Zonal Persistent Disk
is, of course, durable.

00:08:12.770 --> 00:08:14.300
It also supports
snapshots, which

00:08:14.300 --> 00:08:18.400
can be saved to a different
region for geo-redundancy.

00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:19.930
For mission-critical
workloads that

00:08:19.930 --> 00:08:22.630
need to meet very low recovery
point objectives and recovery

00:08:22.630 --> 00:08:25.060
time objectives,
Regional Persistent Disk

00:08:25.060 --> 00:08:28.030
provides a highly
available option.

00:08:28.030 --> 00:08:32.590
Regional PD offers RPO for
single failures and RTO

00:08:32.590 --> 00:08:35.250
less than a minute
at a disk level.

00:08:35.250 --> 00:08:37.230
Of course, the
storage requirements

00:08:37.230 --> 00:08:40.520
may not apply uniformly
to entire workloads.

00:08:40.520 --> 00:08:42.710
Sometimes different
needs within a workload

00:08:42.710 --> 00:08:45.290
are best met by
different disk types.

00:08:45.290 --> 00:08:47.880
For example, for a
SQL server instance

00:08:47.880 --> 00:08:50.420
you might leverage the
higher IOPS, lower latency,

00:08:50.420 --> 00:08:53.390
and lower cost of local
SSD for a temp DB, which

00:08:53.390 --> 00:08:56.330
doesn't need to be
persistent, and have

00:08:56.330 --> 00:08:59.190
the data on a Performance
or even Extreme PD.

00:08:59.190 --> 00:09:02.010
For an analytics pipeline, you
might have source and output

00:09:02.010 --> 00:09:04.103
data on the Google
Cloud Storage,

00:09:04.103 --> 00:09:05.520
while the intermediate
data that's

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:07.200
produced by one stage
of your pipeline

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:11.760
and consumed by another might be
in Persistent Disk or Local SSD

00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:14.440
to achieve higher performance.

00:09:14.440 --> 00:09:16.960
Using different disk
types with an instance

00:09:16.960 --> 00:09:20.560
has interesting
performance implications.

00:09:20.560 --> 00:09:22.780
Let's have a look at
understanding disk performance

00:09:22.780 --> 00:09:25.237
in a bit more detail.

00:09:25.237 --> 00:09:26.695
Persistent Disk
performance depends

00:09:26.695 --> 00:09:30.910
on disk type, disk size,
instance type and size.

00:09:30.910 --> 00:09:33.130
In this example, I have
an N2 standard instance

00:09:33.130 --> 00:09:37.770
with 64 vCPU that can support
a maximum of 100,000 IOPS.

00:09:37.770 --> 00:09:40.920
A Performance PD volume
is sized to 3 terabytes.

00:09:40.920 --> 00:09:45.490
And at 30 IOPS per gigabyte,
that results in 90,000 IOPS.

00:09:45.490 --> 00:09:47.650
If my workload benefits
from having access

00:09:47.650 --> 00:09:50.680
to the full 100,000
IOPS, I could get there

00:09:50.680 --> 00:09:53.770
by increasing the size of
the volume correspondingly.

00:09:53.770 --> 00:09:55.990
On the other hand, if I
were to create a Balanced

00:09:55.990 --> 00:10:01.360
PD of the same size instead, the
volume would have 18,000 IOPS.

00:10:01.360 --> 00:10:03.190
For some existing
workloads, disks

00:10:03.190 --> 00:10:06.220
are sized to each IOPS
requirements, versus fulfilling

00:10:06.220 --> 00:10:07.780
capacity needs.

00:10:07.780 --> 00:10:09.430
For these workloads,
especially if they

00:10:09.430 --> 00:10:13.580
aren't PD standard disks, the
introduction of Balanced PD

00:10:13.580 --> 00:10:16.520
might change the equation.

00:10:16.520 --> 00:10:19.580
Throughput for Performance PD
and Balanced PD is full duplex.

00:10:19.580 --> 00:10:21.890
Workloads can use
1,200 megabytes read

00:10:21.890 --> 00:10:25.160
and 1,200 megabytes
write concurrently.

00:10:25.160 --> 00:10:29.000
On the other hand, IOPS
pull from the same budget.

00:10:29.000 --> 00:10:33.680
So read IOPS plus write IOPS
hit the same 100,000 or 80,000

00:10:33.680 --> 00:10:37.070
limit for Performance PD or
Balanced PD, respectively.

00:10:40.290 --> 00:10:43.200
Disks of the same type
count by total capacity

00:10:43.200 --> 00:10:44.820
regardless of volume.

00:10:44.820 --> 00:10:46.860
One disk of 3
terabyte or two disks

00:10:46.860 --> 00:10:50.530
of the same total capacity
behave the same way.

00:10:50.530 --> 00:10:54.290
In this example, my two disks
add up to the same capacity

00:10:54.290 --> 00:10:56.330
that I previously
provisioned, and I've

00:10:56.330 --> 00:10:59.030
continued to have 90,000 IOPS.

00:10:59.030 --> 00:11:01.580
Under contention, the
IOPS are split evenly

00:11:01.580 --> 00:11:05.690
across the volumes,
regardless of size.

00:11:05.690 --> 00:11:08.450
If your workload has specific
requirements for subcomponents

00:11:08.450 --> 00:11:10.753
or tasks, control
the IOPS consumption

00:11:10.753 --> 00:11:11.670
at the workload level.

00:11:14.200 --> 00:11:17.160
PD disks of different types
consume the same instance level

00:11:17.160 --> 00:11:20.310
IOPS and throughput budget.

00:11:20.310 --> 00:11:22.810
In this example, I've
added a Standard PD disk

00:11:22.810 --> 00:11:24.210
to the instance.

00:11:24.210 --> 00:11:26.020
And now I have to
watch for smaller jobs

00:11:26.020 --> 00:11:28.750
and PD standard consuming
IOPS or throughput

00:11:28.750 --> 00:11:30.960
during spikes for the
Performance PD-bound workload.

00:11:34.290 --> 00:11:37.300
Local SSD and Persistent Disk do
not share in IOPS or throughput

00:11:37.300 --> 00:11:37.800
limit.

00:11:41.552 --> 00:11:43.760
Let's have a look at some
best practices and pitfalls

00:11:43.760 --> 00:11:46.530
that we commonly see.

00:11:46.530 --> 00:11:49.660
Disks smaller than 500
gigabytes have a boost feature.

00:11:49.660 --> 00:11:51.810
This allows them to
boost the performance

00:11:51.810 --> 00:11:55.600
to that of a 500 gigabyte drive
for short periods of time.

00:11:55.600 --> 00:11:57.650
For the most
predictable performance,

00:11:57.650 --> 00:12:01.350
size disks to be at
least 500 gigabytes.

00:12:01.350 --> 00:12:03.870
In larger scale at workloads
and in some workloads that

00:12:03.870 --> 00:12:05.970
migrated from
on-premise, we sometimes

00:12:05.970 --> 00:12:09.120
see Persistent Disk use
as if it weren't durable.

00:12:09.120 --> 00:12:10.980
In other words,
workloads sometimes

00:12:10.980 --> 00:12:14.220
rebuilt data disks for nodes
after instance or node failure.

00:12:14.220 --> 00:12:17.670
And in these cases, seeing
if rebuilding the data disk

00:12:17.670 --> 00:12:20.550
is really needed might result
in substantial performance

00:12:20.550 --> 00:12:21.870
improvements.

00:12:21.870 --> 00:12:24.210
For large nodes, this
can turn minutes or even

00:12:24.210 --> 00:12:27.240
hours of recreating data disks
into seconds of reattaching

00:12:27.240 --> 00:12:30.370
to the existing disk.

00:12:30.370 --> 00:12:33.340
As we just learned,
Local SSD instance limits

00:12:33.340 --> 00:12:36.110
are separate from Persistent
Disk instance limits.

00:12:36.110 --> 00:12:40.540
Pairing it up allows PD to
achieve higher performance.

00:12:40.540 --> 00:12:44.500
For example, using local
SSD as scratch space

00:12:44.500 --> 00:12:48.340
can move IOPS from PD volumes
hitting instance IOPS limits.

00:12:48.340 --> 00:12:54.320
Or run the workload in Local SSD
with a replica or backup in PD.

00:12:54.320 --> 00:12:57.590
Finally, I want to cover peak
versus average performance

00:12:57.590 --> 00:12:58.730
and throttling.

00:12:58.730 --> 00:13:01.280
Workloads with spikes,
we'll see throttled IOPS

00:13:01.280 --> 00:13:03.950
and/or bytes even while
average performance is not

00:13:03.950 --> 00:13:07.320
hitting the maximum of
the instance or volume.

00:13:07.320 --> 00:13:10.710
You'll see this by average
meters showing performance

00:13:10.710 --> 00:13:13.510
below what you would
see as a maximum,

00:13:13.510 --> 00:13:17.290
but potentially peak showing
values that are much higher.

00:13:17.290 --> 00:13:20.470
This is because average meters
cover 1 minute intervals,

00:13:20.470 --> 00:13:23.440
while peak is a
point-in-time measure.

00:13:23.440 --> 00:13:25.623
What this means is that
for these workloads,

00:13:25.623 --> 00:13:27.040
there are often
two different ways

00:13:27.040 --> 00:13:28.960
of achieving better performance.

00:13:28.960 --> 00:13:31.850
You could increase the
IOPS for the volume,

00:13:31.850 --> 00:13:33.700
for example, by
changing capacity,

00:13:33.700 --> 00:13:35.890
disk type, or instance type.

00:13:35.890 --> 00:13:37.570
Or it might be
possible to smooth out

00:13:37.570 --> 00:13:40.480
the spikes of requests
coming from the workload.

00:13:40.480 --> 00:13:43.330
If feasible, the latter can
achieve the same end result

00:13:43.330 --> 00:13:45.460
without increasing
the resources required

00:13:45.460 --> 00:13:47.040
and, hence, the TCO
of the workload.

00:13:50.680 --> 00:13:52.180
And that concludes
our session today

00:13:52.180 --> 00:13:55.690
covering Best Practices for
Block Storage on Google Cloud.

00:13:55.690 --> 00:13:57.310
With Balanced PD
and Extreme PD, we

00:13:57.310 --> 00:14:00.160
have introduced two new disk
types that fundamentally change

00:14:00.160 --> 00:14:03.340
the price/performance
profile for general purpose

00:14:03.340 --> 00:14:05.863
and performance-critical
workloads respectively.

00:14:05.863 --> 00:14:07.780
And together with the
substantial improvements

00:14:07.780 --> 00:14:10.180
in performance with
existing disk types,

00:14:10.180 --> 00:14:12.460
you can now pick the right
disk for your workload,

00:14:12.460 --> 00:14:14.140
matching the
flexibility you already

00:14:14.140 --> 00:14:16.340
had with our VM families.

00:14:16.340 --> 00:14:18.000
Thank you.

